  "response": "I pulled the three IA findings out of the scan and read the underlying fixture files to check the detector's evidence against the source.\n\n## 1. `duplicated_navigation_source` — `crime_00005` · **block merge**\n\n```\nCharge: Duplicated Navigation Source        medium · confidence=0.70\nEvidence:\n  · destination: /settings/billing\n  · src/nav/registry.ts label: Plans\n  · src/nav/sidebar.ts label: Billing\n```\n\nConfirmed in source — `src/nav/sidebar.ts:6` has `{ to: \"/settings/billing\", label: \"Billing\" }`, `src/nav/registry.ts:6` has `{ path: \"/settings/billing\", title: \"Plans\" }`. Two independent arrays, different key names (`to`/`label` vs `path`/`title`), so nothing in TypeScript will ever reconcile them.\n\nThis is the only one of the three that is a *defect generator* rather than a defect. There's no canonical file, no shared type, and the two shapes don't even grep alike — an agent asked to \"rename Billing to Payments\" will find one array and stop. It has already produced the divergence the detector reports.\n\nTwo things make it worse than the score suggests. First, it's the root cause feeding finding #2: two of that finding's seven evidence lines are these nav labels. Second, cross-referencing the three `orphaned_destination` findings (`crime_00034/35/36` — `/account/subscription`, `/team`, `/workspace/members`), the nav sources between them cover exactly two routes out of five. The nav layer is duplicated *and* incomplete, which means \"check the nav\" is not a reliable way for anyone to answer \"what pages exist.\"\n\nFix matches `fix_shape`: one exported nav array, routes derive from it. Small, mechanical, and it collapses part of #2 for free.\n\n## 2. `route_metadata_drift` — `crime_00006` · **warn**\n\n```\nCharge: Route Metadata Drift                medium · confidence=0.65\nEvidence:\n  · route path: /settings/billing      · component: PricingPage\n  · metadata.title: Plans              · <title>: Subscription\n  · nav label in src/nav/registry.ts: Plans\n  · nav label in src/nav/sidebar.ts: Billing\n```\n\nWarn, not block — and the seven evidence lines are not equally load-bearing.\n\nThe part worth acting on is inside `src/routes/settings/billing.tsx` itself: `metadata.title = \"Plans\"` on line 5 versus `<title>Subscription</title>` on line 10. Two title mechanisms in one 14-line file emitting different strings is a user-visible bug in whichever one wins, and it's a one-line fix.\n\nThe rest is softer. \"Route path says `billing`, component is named `PricingPage`\" is a real smell but also a common and defensible convention — URLs are stable contracts, component names are internal concepts. This is the lowest-confidence finding of the three (0.65) and the detector is counting file path and component name as peer \"labels\" alongside actual rendered strings, which inflates the \"4 competing ways\" count. I wouldn't file a `--verdict fp` against it, because the title disagreement is genuine, but I'd treat the naming half as advisory.\n\n`blast_radius` is 0 and nothing here breaks at runtime. Comment on it; don't gate on it. Fixing #1 removes two of its evidence lines anyway.\n\n## 3. `docs_code_drift` — `crime_00030` · **warn** (but promote to a hard gate)\n\n```\nCharge: Docs-Code Drift                     low · confidence=0.90\nEvidence:\n  · docs/teams.md:5 → ./setup.md (not found)\n```\n\nVerified — `docs/teams.md:5` reads `See the [setup guide](./setup.md) to invite the first members.` and there is no `docs/setup.md`.\n\nLowest severity, highest confidence (0.90), and the only one of the three that is a binary fact rather than a heuristic judgement: the file is on disk or it isn't. Zero false-positive surface. A dead relative link in a docs file with no importers isn't worth failing a build over as standing debt, so: warn.\n\nBut this is the finding to promote into CI, precisely *because* it can't misfire. `crimes scan --changed --fail-on low` will catch the moment a PR adds a broken link or deletes a linked file — which is the case where it genuinely should block, since the PR author has the context to fix it and nobody else will.\n\n## Summary\n\n| Finding | Verdict | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `duplicated_navigation_source` | **block** | Two unreconcilable sources of truth, already diverged, generates future drift |\n| `route_metadata_drift` | **warn** | Real title conflict inside one file; naming half is conventional, confidence 0.65 |\n| `docs_code_drift` | **warn** | Deterministic and trivially fixable — gate it on changed files, not on legacy debt |\n\nNothing here is an **ignore**. All three are cheap, and they're mutually reinforcing: fix the nav single-source-of-truth first and finding #2 shrinks to a one-line title fix.\n\nOne caveat on scope — the scan reports `1 file matched an \"exclude\" pattern and was never read` (`.crimes/suppressions.json`). That's the suppressions file itself, so it doesn't affect this triage, but findings in excluded paths are absent rather than zero.",
